-    "content": "# [Guide] Three-Surface Model Context Protocol (MCP) Server Architecture\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Model Context Protocol (MCP) allows Large Language Models (LLMs) and autonomous engineering agents to interact with development platforms. However, conventional tracker integrations (such as legacy Jira REST bridges and generic CRUD wrappers) expose raw REST endpoints 1:1 as MCP tools. This naive approach results in 70 to 120 bloated tools consuming 15,000 to 25,000 context tokens before the agent begins reasoning, causing catastrophic context exhaustion and multi-turn thrashing.\n\n\ud83d\udc4dkanban rejects the CRUD-wrapper pattern. Instead, it implements an **Intent-Driven Architecture** organized into **three isolated streamable surfaces** (`2025-06-18` protocol specification), exposing exactly 24 cohesive tools (9 `/mcp/agent`, 3 `/mcp/docs`, 12 `/mcp/admin`) designed specifically for autonomous agent cognition.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"scope-tiers\"></a>\n## The Three Isolated MCP Surfaces\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph Hub[\"\ud83d\udc4dkanban Multi-Surface MCP Server\"]\n        Agent[\"/mcp/agent<br/>- 9 Execution & QA Tools<br/>- Token budget: <= 1,850 tokens<br/>- Zero Admin / Delete permissions\"]\n        Docs[\"/mcp/docs<br/>- 3 Knowledge & Spec Tools<br/>- Token budget: <= 1,350 tokens<br/>- Search, Create, Update Docs\"]\n        Admin[\"/mcp/admin<br/>- 12 Lifecycle & Ops Tools<br/>- Token budget: <= 2,800 tokens<br/>- Mandatory AAL2 Protection<br/>- 2-Stage Destructive Confirmation\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Agents[\"Target Consumers\"]\n        CodingAgents[\"Autonomous Coding & QA Agents<br/>(Cursor, Claude Code, Antigravity, Codex)\"]\n        DocAgents[\"Documentation & Knowledge Bots\"]\n        AdminOperators[\"DevOps & Administrative Operators\"]\n    end\n\n    CodingAgents --> Agent\n    DocAgents --> Docs\n    AdminOperators --> Admin\n```\n\n![MCP Access Studio and Key Configuration](images/13-mcp-access-studio.png)\n\n![MCP Level of Detail Tiers Studio](images/21-mcp-lod-tiers-studio.png)\n\n### 1. `/mcp/agent` (9 Execution and QA Tools)\nDedicated to autonomous software development, testing, and QA agents (such as Claude Code, Antigravity, and Cursor).\n- **Security Constraint:** Strictly limited by the caller's role. Contains zero administrative, destructive, or project-deletion capabilities.\n- **Token Efficiency:** The entire tool manifest consumes only 1,759 tokens, comfortably fitting within the strict 1,850-token specification ceiling.\n- **Intent Consolidation:** Replaces multi-step CRUD sequences with atomic actions like `begin_work` (claims ticket, starts sprint, links active cycle, posts plan) and `complete_work` (verifies blockers, records QA proof, marks done).\n\n### 2. `/mcp/docs` (3 Knowledge and Specification Tools)\nDedicated to technical documentation, architecture research, and specification authoring.\n- **Capabilities:** Document creation, markdown search, and content updates (`search`, `create`, `update`).\n- **Token Efficiency:** Tool manifest utilizes under 800 tokens against a 1,350-token budget ceiling.\n\n### 3. `/mcp/admin` (12 Governance and Lifecycle Tools)\nDedicated to platform administrators and privileged automation operators.\n- **Capabilities:** Project provisioning, member and group access binding, workflow transition helper configuration, AI runner setup, and system health checks.\n- **AAL2 Protection:** Every invocation requires Authenticator Assurance Level 2 credentials.\n- **Two-Stage Destructive Safety:** Dangerous operations (`destructive_lifecycle`) require a two-stage process: first requesting a blast-radius preview and receiving a short-lived cryptographic confirmation token, then explicitly confirming execution with that token.\n\n---\n\n## Token Optimization and Serializer Architecture\n\nAutonomous agents run faster and make fewer mistakes when data payloads are clean, predictable, and compact.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    Request[\"Agent Invocation<br/>e.g. read_ticket(identifier='KAN-42')\"] --> Auth[\"Auth & Scope Check\"]\n    Auth --> Resolver[\"EntityResolver<br/>(Translate KAN-42 to UUID)\"]\n    Resolver --> DB[\"PostgreSQL Engine<br/>FORCE RLS Active\"]\n    DB --> LODFilter[\"LOD Filter<br/>(summary / standard / full)\"]\n    LODFilter --> Dumper[\"InlineSafeDumper<br/>Strip nulls, compact YAML\"]\n    Dumper --> Response[\"Streamable Response<br/>(-35% Tokens vs JSON)\"]\n```\n\n### `InlineSafeDumper` Compact YAML Output\nWhile standard MCP servers return formatted JSON, \ud83d\udc4dkanban uses a specialized YAML serializer (`mcp/yaml_out.py`):\n- Compact, human-readable YAML representation.\n- Strips null values, empty strings, and default boolean flags.\n- Automatically redacts sensitive fields (passwords, tokens, keys).\n- **Result:** Reduces payload size by 35% compared to raw JSON, saving thousands of tokens per conversation.\n\n### `EntityResolver` Human-Readable Identifiers\nAgents reason far better about human identifiers (`KAN-42`, `CORE-101`, `infra-docs`) than opaque UUIDs (`01932e6b-7341-789a-bc01-123456789abc`). The `EntityResolver` automatically translates between human keys and internal database UUIDs at the MCP boundary.\n\n### Level-of-Detail (`lod`) Filtering\nSearch and read tools support a progressive disclosure parameter:\n- `lod: \"summary\"`: Returns identifier, title, state, and priority (~40 tokens).\n- `lod: \"standard\"`: Returns summary plus description and primary assignees (~200 tokens).\n- `lod: \"full\"`: Returns complete object graph, all comments, activity history, and access manifests.\n\n---\n\n## MCP Access Studio and Key Management\n\nUsers and administrators configure MCP integrations through the visual Access Studio at `/admin/mcp` and `/settings/mcp`.\n\n![MCP Access Studio and Key Configuration](images/13-mcp-access-studio.png)\n\n### Studio Features\n- **Granular Tool Toggles:** Enable or disable individual MCP tools per key or per surface.\n- **Hierarchical Scopes and Scope Badges:** Restrict tokens to `Global`, `Workspace`, or single `Project` scopes with distinct visual badges indicating authorization level.\n- **RFC 8628 Device Flow (`/activate`):** Fast terminal onboarding allowing agents to authenticate via browser activation code.\n- **Copyable Configuration Snippets:** Real-time generation of configuration snippets for Claude Desktop (`claude_desktop_config.json`), Google Antigravity (`agy`), Cursor, and OpenAI Codex.\n\n![MCP Access Studio Scope Badges](images/59-mcp-scope-badge.png)\n\n![MCP Mint Key and Client Snippets Modal](images/58-mcp-client-snippets-mint.png)\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"oauth-21--pkce\"></a>\n<a id=\"oauth-21-pkce\"></a>\n## Admin OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio (`/admin/oauth`)\n\nIn addition to static API keys, \ud83d\udc4dkanban provides an enterprise OAuth 2.1 authorization server with mandatory PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange with `S256` challenge), accessible at `/admin/oauth`.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph OAuthGovernance[\"OAuth 2.1 & PKCE Architecture\"]\n        ClientApp[\"Third-Party Client / IDE Agent<br/>(Cursor, Claude Desktop, Antigravity)\"]\n        AuthServer[\"\ud83d\udc4dkanban OAuth 2.1 Server (/admin/oauth)\"]\n        TokenEndpoint[\"Token Minting Endpoint (/api/v1/oauth/token)\"]\n        AdminAudit[\"Audit & Revocation Stream\"]\n\n        ClientApp -->|1. Authorization Code Request + code_challenge (S256)| AuthServer\n        AuthServer -->|2. User Authenticates & Approves Scopes| ClientApp\n        ClientApp -->|3. POST /oauth/token + code_verifier| TokenEndpoint\n        TokenEndpoint -->|4. Cryptographic S256 Verification| TokenEndpoint\n        TokenEndpoint -->>|5. Mint Scoped Bearer JWT + Refresh Token| ClientApp\n        TokenEndpoint -->|6. Append Registration & Issuance Record| AdminAudit\n    end\n```\n\n![Admin OAuth Client Governance Studio](images/42-admin-oauth-governance.png)\n\n### Key OAuth Governance Capabilities:\n1. **Registered OAuth 2.1 Clients:** View, register, and manage client applications with designated redirect URIs and client secrets.\n2. **Mandatory PKCE (`S256`):** Disallows plain code challenges, protecting public clients against authorization code interception attacks.\n3. **Granular Scopes:** Scopes are scoped strictly (`mcp:read`, `mcp:write`, `agent:execute`, `docs:read`, `admin:full`).\n4. **Immediate Client Revocation:** Administrators can revoke compromised OAuth clients or reset client secrets with zero system downtime.\n"
+    "content": "# [Guide] Three-Surface Model Context Protocol (MCP) Server Architecture\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Model Context Protocol (MCP) allows Large Language Models (LLMs) and autonomous engineering agents to interact with development platforms. However, conventional tracker integrations (such as legacy Jira REST bridges and generic CRUD wrappers) expose raw REST endpoints 1:1 as MCP tools. This naive approach results in 70 to 120 bloated tools consuming 15,000 to 25,000 context tokens before the agent begins reasoning, causing catastrophic context exhaustion and multi-turn thrashing.\n\n\ud83d\udc4dkanban rejects the CRUD-wrapper pattern. Instead, it implements an **Intent-Driven Architecture** organized into **three isolated streamable surfaces** (`2025-06-18` protocol specification), exposing exactly 25 cohesive tools (10 `/mcp/agent`, 3 `/mcp/docs`, 12 `/mcp/admin`) designed specifically for autonomous agent cognition.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"scope-tiers\"></a>\n## The Three Isolated MCP Surfaces\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph Hub[\"\ud83d\udc4dkanban Multi-Surface MCP Server\"]\n        Agent[\"/mcp/agent - 10 Execution & QA Tools - Token budget: <= 1,850 tokens - Zero Admin / Delete permissions\"]\n        Docs[\"/mcp/docs - 3 Knowledge & Spec Tools - Token budget: <= 1,350 tokens - Search, Create, Update Docs\"]\n        Admin[\"/mcp/admin - 12 Lifecycle & Ops Tools - Token budget: <= 2,800 tokens - Mandatory AAL2 Protection - 2-Stage Destructive Confirmation\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Agents[\"Target Consumers\"]\n        CodingAgents[\"Autonomous Coding & QA Agents (Cursor, Claude Code, Antigravity, Codex)\"]\n        DocAgents[\"Documentation & Knowledge Bots\"]\n        AdminOperators[\"DevOps & Administrative Operators\"]\n    end\n\n    CodingAgents --> Agent\n    DocAgents --> Docs\n    AdminOperators --> Admin\n```\n\n![MCP Access Studio and Key Minting Modal](images/slice3-mcp-access-studio-mint-modal.png)\n\n![MCP Config Snippet and Client Setup Modal](images/slice3-mcp-config-snippet-modal.png)\n\n### 1. `/mcp/agent` (10 Execution and QA Tools)\nDedicated to autonomous software development, testing, and QA agents (such as Claude Code, Antigravity, and Cursor).\n- **Security Constraint:** Strictly limited by the caller's role. Contains zero administrative, destructive, or project-deletion capabilities.\n- **Token Efficiency:** The entire tool manifest consumes only 1,759 tokens, comfortably fitting within the strict 1,850-token specification ceiling.\n- **Intent Consolidation:** Replaces multi-step CRUD sequences with atomic actions like `begin_work` (claims ticket, starts sprint, links active cycle, posts plan), `complete_work` (verifies blockers, records QA proof, marks done), and `update_workpad` (persists intermediate progress).\n\n### 2. `/mcp/docs` (3 Knowledge and Specification Tools)\nDedicated to technical documentation, architecture research, and specification authoring.\n- **Capabilities:** Document creation, markdown search, and content updates (`search`, `create`, `update`).\n- **Token Efficiency:** Tool manifest utilizes under 800 tokens against a 1,350-token budget ceiling.\n\n### 3. `/mcp/admin` (12 Governance and Lifecycle Tools)\nDedicated to platform administrators and privileged automation operators.\n- **Capabilities:** Project provisioning, member and group access binding, workflow transition helper configuration, AI runner setup, and system health checks.\n- **AAL2 Protection:** Every invocation requires Authenticator Assurance Level 2 credentials.\n- **Two-Stage Destructive Safety:** Dangerous operations (`destructive_lifecycle`) require a two-stage process: first requesting a blast-radius preview and receiving a short-lived cryptographic confirmation token, then explicitly confirming execution with that token.\n\n---\n\n## Token Optimization and Serializer Architecture\n\nAutonomous agents run faster and make fewer mistakes when data payloads are clean, predictable, and compact.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    Request[\"Agent Invocation - e.g. read_ticket(identifier='KAN-42')\"] --> Auth[\"Auth & Scope Check\"]\n    Auth --> Resolver[\"EntityResolver - Translate KAN-42 to UUID\"]\n    Resolver --> DB[\"PostgreSQL Engine - FORCE RLS Active\"]\n    DB --> LODFilter[\"LOD Filter - summary / standard / full\"]\n    LODFilter --> Dumper[\"InlineSafeDumper - Strip nulls, compact YAML\"]\n    Dumper --> Response[\"Streamable Response - (-35% Tokens vs JSON)\"]\n```\n\n### `InlineSafeDumper` Compact YAML Output\nWhile standard MCP servers return formatted JSON, \ud83d\udc4dkanban uses a specialized YAML serializer (`mcp/yaml_out.py`):\n- Compact, human-readable YAML representation.\n- Strips null values, empty strings, and default boolean flags.\n- Automatically redacts sensitive fields (passwords, tokens, keys).\n- **Result:** Reduces payload size by 35% compared to raw JSON, saving thousands of tokens per conversation.\n\n### `EntityResolver` Human-Readable Identifiers\nAgents reason far better about human identifiers (`KAN-42`, `CORE-101`, `infra-docs`) than opaque UUIDs (`01932e6b-7341-789a-bc01-123456789abc`). The `EntityResolver` automatically translates between human keys and internal database UUIDs at the MCP boundary.\n\n### Level-of-Detail (`lod`) Filtering\nSearch and read tools support a progressive disclosure parameter:\n- `lod: \"summary\"`: Returns identifier, title, state, and priority (~40 tokens).\n- `lod: \"standard\"`: Returns summary plus description and primary assignees (~200 tokens).\n- `lod: \"full\"`: Returns complete object graph, all comments, activity history, and access manifests.\n\n---\n\n## MCP Access Studio and Key Management\n\nUsers and administrators configure MCP integrations through the visual Access Studio at `/settings/mcp` and `/admin/mcp`.\n\n![MCP Access Studio and Key Minting Modal](images/slice3-mcp-access-studio-mint-modal.png)\n\n### Studio Features\n- **Granular Tool Toggles:** Enable or disable individual MCP tools per key or per surface.\n- **Hierarchical Scopes and Scope Badges:** Restrict tokens to `Global`, `Workspace`, `Project`, or `Personal` scopes with distinct visual badges indicating authorization level.\n- **Capability Bitmasks:** Enforce monotonic capability boundaries between `0` (deny-all) and `255` (full privileges).\n- **CIDR Restrictions:** Limit key invocation to specific IP subnets (e.g. `127.0.0.1/32`, `10.0.0.0/8`).\n- **RFC 8628 Device Flow (`/activate`):** Fast terminal onboarding allowing agents to authenticate via browser activation code.\n- **Copyable Configuration Snippets:** Real-time generation of configuration snippets for Claude Desktop, Google Antigravity (`agy`), Cursor, and OpenAI Codex.\n\n![MCP Access Studio Scope Badges](images/59-mcp-scope-badge.png)\n\n![MCP Mint Key and Client Snippets Modal](images/58-mcp-client-snippets-mint.png)\n\n![Connect OAuth App Modal](images/slice3-connect-oauth-modal.png)\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"oauth-21--pkce\"></a>\n<a id=\"oauth-21-pkce\"></a>\n## Admin OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio (`/admin/oauth`)\n\nIn addition to static API keys, \ud83d\udc4dkanban provides an enterprise OAuth 2.1 authorization server with mandatory PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange with `S256` challenge), accessible at `/admin/oauth`.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph OAuthGovernance[\"OAuth 2.1 & PKCE Architecture\"]\n        ClientApp[\"Third-Party Client / IDE Agent (Cursor, Claude Desktop, Antigravity)\"]\n        AuthServer[\"\ud83d\udc4dkanban OAuth 2.1 Server (/admin/oauth)\"]\n        TokenEndpoint[\"Token Minting Endpoint (/oauth/token)\"]\n        AdminAudit[\"Audit & Revocation Stream\"]\n\n        ClientApp -->|1. Authorization Code Request + code_challenge S256| AuthServer\n        AuthServer -->|2. User Authenticates & Approves Scopes| ClientApp\n        ClientApp -->|3. POST /oauth/token + code_verifier| TokenEndpoint\n        TokenEndpoint -->|4. Cryptographic S256 Verification| TokenEndpoint\n        TokenEndpoint -->|5. Mint Scoped Bearer JWT + Refresh Token| ClientApp\n        TokenEndpoint -->|6. Append Registration & Issuance Record| AdminAudit\n    end\n```\n\n![OAuth Client Governance Studio Bulk Purge](images/slice3-oauth-governance-bulk-purge.png)\n\n![OAuth Client Governance Studio Paginated 500 Clients](images/slice3-oauth-governance-paginated-500.png)\n\n![Admin OAuth Client Governance Studio](images/42-admin-oauth-governance.png)\n\n### Key OAuth Governance Capabilities\n1. **Registered OAuth 2.1 Clients:** View, register, and manage client applications with designated redirect URIs, client types (`public` or `confidential`), and authentication methods (`none`, `client_secret_post`, `client_secret_basic`).\n2. **Mandatory PKCE (`S256`):** Disallows plain code challenges, protecting public clients against authorization code interception attacks.\n3. **State Parameter Ceiling (`MAX_STATE_LEN = 4096`):** Supports large serialized state objects (up to 4,096 characters) required by cloud AI assistants like Google Gemini.\n4. **Granular Scopes:** Scopes are scoped strictly (`agent`, `docs`, `admin`, `admin:read`, `admin:write`, `admin:destructive`, `read:issues`, `write:issues`).\n5. **Immediate Client Revocation & Bulk Purge:** Administrators can revoke compromised OAuth clients or execute single-transaction bulk purges (`POST /api/v1/admin/oauth-clients/bulk-delete`) to remove hundreds of stale Dynamic Client Registration (DCR) clients without disrupting active client sessions.\n\n---\n\n## Step-by-Step Integration Guides\n\n### Guide A: Connecting Gemini App, Claude, Cursor & CLI via OAuth 2.1\n\n#### 1. Google Gemini Connected Apps\n1. Open the \ud83d\udc4dkanban MCP Access Studio (`/settings/mcp`) and click **Connect OAuth App**.\n2. Select the **Gemini App** tab to view your MCP OAuth Resource URL (e.g. `https://kanban.example.com/mcp/agent`).\n3. In Google Gemini (gemini.google.com), navigate to **Settings > Connected Apps > Add Custom MCP Server**.\n4. Enter the Resource URL and complete the OAuth 2.1 PKCE authorization flow in the browser popup.\n\n#### 2. Claude Desktop & Claude Code\n1. In the **Connect OAuth App** modal, select the **Claude Code** tab.\n2. For Claude Code CLI, run the auto-generated command:\n   ```bash\n   claude mcp add kanban https://kanban.example.com/mcp/agent\n   ```\n3. For Claude Desktop, add the server configuration to `claude_desktop_config.json`:\n   ```json\n   {\n     \"mcpServers\": {\n       \"kanban\": {\n         \"url\": \"https://kanban.example.com/mcp/agent\"\n       }\n     }\n   }\n   ```\n\n#### 3. Cursor & VS Code\n1. In Cursor, open **Settings > Features > MCP**.\n2. Add a new MCP server with Server Type `SSE` (or HTTP) and URL `https://kanban.example.com/mcp/agent`.\n3. When prompted, complete the OAuth 2.1 authorization in your default browser.\n\n#### 4. Antigravity & OpenAI Codex CLI\n1. For Google Antigravity CLI, run:\n   ```bash\n   agy mcp add kanban https://kanban.example.com/mcp/agent --auth oauth\n   ```\n2. For OpenAI Codex CLI, run:\n   ```bash\n   codex mcp add kanban https://kanban.example.com/mcp/agent --auth oauth\n   ```\n\n---\n\n### Guide B: Minting Scoped MCP API Keys\n\n1. Navigate to `/settings/mcp` (or `/admin/mcp` for administrative keys).\n2. Click **+ Mint MCP Key** to open the creation modal.\n3. **Configure Key Metadata:**\n   - **Key Name / Label:** Descriptive name (e.g. `CI Bot Runner`).\n   - **Role:** Select `Personal` (for user-bound keys) or `Service Bot` (for headless automation).\n   - **Scope Tier:** Choose `Project`, `Workspace`, or `Global`. For project keys, select the target project.\n   - **Surface:** Select `/mcp/agent` or `/mcp/docs`.\n   - **Expiration:** Choose a preset (30, 60, 90 days, 1 year) or `Unlimited` (requires checking the risk acknowledgment checkbox).\n   - **Capability Mask:** Select a preset (`Full Admin: 255`, `Standard: 31`, `Read-Only: 1`, `Deny-All: 0`) or configure individual bitmask flags.\n   - **CIDR Allowlist:** Optionally specify comma-separated allowed IPv4/IPv6 subnets (e.g. `192.168.1.0/24`).\n   - **Tool Toggles:** Deselect specific tools to disable them for this key.\n4. Click **Mint Key** and copy the secret token immediately. The raw token is write-only and will never be displayed again.\n\n---\n\n### Guide C: Configuring Headless Workload OIDC (GitHub Actions / GitLab CI / Kubernetes)\n\n1. Open `/admin/mcp` and navigate to the **Workload Identity Federation** section.\n2. Click **+ Add Workload Policy** to open the policy creation drawer.\n3. Select a provider preset or enter custom settings:\n   - **GitHub Actions Preset:** Issuer `https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com`, Subject pattern `repo:my-org/my-repo:ref:refs/heads/main`.\n   - **GitLab CI Preset:** Issuer `https://gitlab.com`, Subject pattern `project_path:my-group/my-project:ref_type:branch:ref:main`.\n   - **Kubernetes Preset:** Issuer `https://kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local`, Subject pattern `system:serviceaccount:default:ci-runner`.\n4. Configure required claims, target surface (`/mcp/agent`), allowed project scopes, capability mask, and token TTL (`max_ttl_seconds <= 86400`).\n5. Save the policy. Headless workloads can now exchange native OIDC tokens for ephemeral MCP tokens via `POST /oauth/token` without static passwords.\n\n---\n\n### Guide D: Governing and Purging OAuth 2.1 Clients\n\n1. Navigate to `/admin/oauth` to access the OAuth Client Governance Studio.\n2. **Inspect Clients:** Use filter pills (`All`, `Active`, `Suspended`, `Revoked`) or search by client name or ID.\n3. **Manage Lifecycle:**\n   - Click **Inspect** to review client registration metadata, redirect URIs, and scopes.\n   - Click **Suspend** to temporarily disable token exchange for a client.\n   - Click **Revoke** to permanently invalidate a client and all associated refresh tokens.\n4. **Single-Transaction Bulk Purge:**\n   - To clean up stale Dynamic Client Registration (DCR) clients created during automated testing or evaluation, click **Purge Stale Clients**.\n   - Review the count of unused clients (clients without active refresh tokens or grants).\n   - Confirm the operation to purge up to thousands of stale clients atomically in a single database transaction.\n\n![Device Activation Page](images/slice3-device-activate-page.png)\n"
